1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,040
Speaker:
A year ago, AI was a feature

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:04,160
Speaker:
every SaaS product had to add,

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:05,900
Speaker:
like an assistant or an MCP

4
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:06,759
Speaker:
server.

5
00:00:06,759 --> 00:00:10,119
Speaker:
Now it's the thing replacing
them, and that's breaking the

6
00:00:10,119 --> 00:00:12,560
Speaker:
SaaS business model fast.

7
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,960
Speaker:
Traditional SaaS has four parts.

8
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:17,800
Speaker:
Building software used to be
expensive and hard.

9
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,519
Speaker:
Now coding agents make it nearly
free.

10
00:00:20,519 --> 00:00:22,079
Speaker:
Running software at scale is

11
00:00:22,079 --> 00:00:23,839
Speaker:
expensive, demanding, and

12
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:24,679
Speaker:
fragile.

13
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:27,920
Speaker:
But most users run at a small
scale, and that's now nearly

14
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,559
Speaker:
free to answering users and
fixing bugs used to take a

15
00:00:31,559 --> 00:00:33,399
Speaker:
support team and engineers.

16
00:00:33,399 --> 00:00:37,200
Speaker:
Now agents answer questions and
fix bugs automatically.

17
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,640
Speaker:
And the product vision.

18
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:41,759
Speaker:
It comes from a founder and a
team who obsess over a problem

19
00:00:41,759 --> 00:00:44,880
Speaker:
until they understand the
solution better than anyone.

20
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,600
Speaker:
That part hasn't changed.

21
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,840
Speaker:
AI can't recreate taste.

22
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,479
Speaker:
Buy versus build used to be a

23
00:00:50,479 --> 00:00:52,020
Speaker:
question only big companies

24
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:52,719
Speaker:
asked.

25
00:00:52,719 --> 00:00:55,200
Speaker:
Now solo creators are asking it
too.

26
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,439
Speaker:
It's not only the monthly bill,
it's paying for one hundred

27
00:00:58,439 --> 00:01:00,399
Speaker:
features to only use three.

28
00:01:00,399 --> 00:01:02,420
Speaker:
Building is becoming the default

29
00:01:02,420 --> 00:01:03,960
Speaker:
and building is becoming

30
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:04,840
Speaker:
cloning.

31
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:08,519
Speaker:
Given agent access to a product
and it copies the whole thing.

32
00:01:08,519 --> 00:01:11,560
Speaker:
But a clone only captures the
tip of the iceberg.

33
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:13,680
Speaker:
The vision, taste, constraints

34
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,200
Speaker:
and opinions of the solution are

35
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,200
Speaker:
lost and the user ends up with a

36
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:18,319
Speaker:
worse version.

37
00:01:18,319 --> 00:01:22,079
Speaker:
They do it because they want
three things ownership to use

38
00:01:22,079 --> 00:01:26,239
Speaker:
their own AI subscription and to
shape it to their own needs.

39
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:28,040
Speaker:
Introducing Super Grid, a

40
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,040
Speaker:
desktop IDE for creators and

41
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:30,959
Speaker:
builders.

42
00:01:30,959 --> 00:01:32,239
Speaker:
Here's how it works.

43
00:01:32,239 --> 00:01:35,879
Speaker:
First, you connect muse code
through ACP so the agent runs on

44
00:01:35,879 --> 00:01:37,439
Speaker:
your own subscription.

45
00:01:37,439 --> 00:01:40,200
Speaker:
Then you connect your own
Cloudflare account so everything

46
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,359
Speaker:
is deployed and owned by you.

47
00:01:42,359 --> 00:01:45,920
Speaker:
Each workspace you create is its
own code repo, and it ships with

48
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:50,560
Speaker:
a database, events, workflows,
analytics, and a media library.

49
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,280
Speaker:
Apps are the main part of a
workspace, and they're built on

50
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:54,560
Speaker:
top of that core.

51
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:55,879
Speaker:
You can build your own or

52
00:01:55,879 --> 00:01:57,400
Speaker:
install apps from other builders

53
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,480
Speaker:
and founders.

54
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:01,480
Speaker:
The app goes straight into your
repo so you get their complete

55
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:03,439
Speaker:
solution, not a clone.

56
00:02:03,439 --> 00:02:05,379
Speaker:
Apps can be internal tools like

57
00:02:05,379 --> 00:02:06,879
Speaker:
an ad designer built on muse

58
00:02:06,879 --> 00:02:08,360
Speaker:
image or deployed with your

59
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:10,120
Speaker:
site, like a blog forms or

60
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:11,280
Speaker:
landing pages.

61
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:14,800
Speaker:
Each app can have its own data
model, components, pages,

62
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:16,639
Speaker:
routes, and back end code.

63
00:02:16,639 --> 00:02:18,800
Speaker:
It can add admin screens for
your team.

64
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:22,949
Speaker:
Hook into the core systems and
expose its features over MCP, so

65
00:02:22,949 --> 00:02:25,050
Speaker:
muse can use the app natively.

66
00:02:25,050 --> 00:02:28,569
Speaker:
That makes every app a small
product with its own front end

67
00:02:28,569 --> 00:02:31,490
Speaker:
and back end, built to fit into
what's already there.

68
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:33,069
Speaker:
The benefit of traditional SaaS

69
00:02:33,069 --> 00:02:34,729
Speaker:
is that updates and fixes are

70
00:02:34,729 --> 00:02:36,610
Speaker:
easy to ship, and everyone gets

71
00:02:36,610 --> 00:02:37,250
Speaker:
them.

72
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:40,370
Speaker:
But with ownership, when
everyone has their own copy,

73
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:42,250
Speaker:
that ease disappears.

74
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:44,689
Speaker:
Muse code is what makes this
possible.

75
00:02:44,689 --> 00:02:47,930
Speaker:
It can extend apps, make
changes, and merge updates from

76
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:49,569
Speaker:
the builder seamlessly.

77
00:02:49,569 --> 00:02:52,449
Speaker:
It checks every update for
security issues and fixes

78
00:02:52,449 --> 00:02:57,050
Speaker:
anything that breaks Muse code
becomes the CTO for every user.

79
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:00,610
Speaker:
It's what makes owning the code
possible in this new SaaS model.

80
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:03,610
Speaker:
Platforms like this could exist
in every industry.

81
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:06,810
Speaker:
Experts and founders could build
solutions that get installed and

82
00:03:06,810 --> 00:03:08,569
Speaker:
owned instead of cloned.

83
00:03:08,569 --> 00:03:09,770
Speaker:
They'd focus on solving the

84
00:03:09,770 --> 00:03:10,889
Speaker:
problem, not on the

85
00:03:10,889 --> 00:03:12,530
Speaker:
infrastructure, and still get

86
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:14,009
Speaker:
paid and build a real business

87
00:03:14,009 --> 00:03:15,090
Speaker:
around it.

88
00:03:15,090 --> 00:03:18,330
Speaker:
SaaS isn't going away, but it is
changing.

89
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:21,210
Speaker:
The code becomes part of the
product that's shipped and owned

90
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:22,490
Speaker:
by the user.

91
00:03:22,490 --> 00:03:25,610
Speaker:
Products built by the people who
understand the problem best,

92
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:29,090
Speaker:
shaped by the people who use
them, and powered by the AI they

93
00:03:29,090 --> 00:03:30,330
Speaker:
already pay for.
